<template><span class="eyebrow">04 / AN ONGOING EXPERIMENT</span><h1>Practice with curiosity.<br>Measure with care.</h1><p class="lead">Human Fluid is a notebook for reasoning strategies.<br>Train strategies. Test transfer.</p><div class="reading-column"><section><h2>The idea</h2><p>Work through unfamiliar problems, test an assumption, and see a concrete explanation. Varied practice, fading hints, and delayed retrieval are our training hypothesis. This application is designed to help evaluate that hypothesis; its effectiveness has not been established.</p></section><section><h2>What changes, and what we measure</h2><ol><li>Performance on practiced formats.</li><li>Retention on fresh, delayed, unaided problems.</li><li>Transfer to a reserved presentation or structure.</li><li>Performance on an entirely untrained family.</li><li>Change on an independent, psychometrically supported reasoning assessment.</li></ol><p>The first three are MVP outcomes. The built-in probe tests an exploratory scheduling presentation of relational ordering. It is related-task presentation transfer. No independent untrained-family or psychometric assessment is currently included. Neither practice gains nor this probe establishes a change in fluid intelligence or general intelligence.</p></section><section><h2>RL-inspired, honestly</h2><p>The application borrows verified reasoning environments and feedback ideas from reinforcement learning. Its scheduler is a transparent rule policy, not a learned reinforcement-learning tutor. It favors broad coverage, due review, and conservative advancement from independent first attempts. Hints and worked examples do not count as independent success. You can change practice levels in Settings; checks stay fixed.</p></section><section><h2>A short evidence note</h2><p>A <a href="https://doi.org/10.3758/s13423-024-02466-8" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2024 meta-analysis by Rodas and colleagues</a> reported small working-memory improvements but no significant average fluid-intelligence improvement from cognitive training. <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8944825/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Callaway and colleagues’ planning-tutor research</a> motivates teaching cognitive actions and studying transfer. It does not validate this application.</p></section><section><h2>Where the problems come from</h2><p>Dependency reasoning and logical consistency use actual <a href="https://github.com/open-thought/reasoning-gym" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Reasoning Gym</a> 0.1.25 generators, independently verified by directed-cycle detection and exhaustive truth assignments. Reasoning Gym was developed for model reasoning, not validated as a human fluid-intelligence curriculum. Ordering, induction, and hypothesis testing use our own bounded, solver-verified models. Induction and hypothesis testing share a rule grammar, so their outcomes are related.</p><p>Imported items are generated into a vetted bank with structural split checks. Native items use bounded generation. Each released explanation includes provenance. A finite rule set is always stated; we never claim examples identify a unique function among all possible functions.</p></section><section><h2>Your notebook, your data</h2><p>No registration, tracking pixels, or advertising analytics. A random HttpOnly cookie connects you to progress stored on this server. Clearing the cookie loses automatic access to the profile; export your notebook first. Restore imports history as unverified data, without transferring identity or AI permissions.</p><p>AI is optional, disabled by default, and available only with an owner-issued invitation. An explicit Send action submits the problem, your answer, explanation, and recent thread to the configured provider. Your scratchpad stays out unless you opt in. Local inference runs on the backend host.</p><RouterLink class="button" to="/settings">Manage your data & AI access →</RouterLink></section></div></template>
